WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.850
From the outside, Asha's bottleneck looks like going live, not selling.

2
00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:10.875
Public posts mention contracted revenue piling up, and more demand than the team can onboard.

3
00:00:10.875 --> 00:00:19.600
My guess is that every new practice needs someone to turn its scripts, hours, provider lists and protocols into agent settings by hand.

4
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:21.900
This prototype tests that guess.

5
00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:29.500
It compiles a practice's intake packet into an agent config, checks it, and leaves a person only the exceptions.

6
00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:34.500
Plain code handles anything that must be exact and repeatable.

7
00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:38.650
The compiler parses five packet files into a typed config.

8
00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:42.100
The validator runs nine deterministic rules.

9
00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:47.075
A semantic check looks for an FAQ answer that contradicts the call script.

10
00:00:47.075 --> 00:00:50.650
That is the only place a language model is allowed.

11
00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:53.550
Everything ends up as a list of issues.

12
00:00:53.550 --> 00:01:00.550
The later stages, repair, review, smoke test and export, are designed in the PRD but not built yet.

13
00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:04.000
There's no real practice data anywhere.

14
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:07.275
A seeded generator writes six fake practices:

15
00:01:07.275 --> 00:01:13.300
internal medicine, cardiology, pulmonology, urgent care and a multispecialty group.

16
00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:18.275
Each packet ships a defects file, the answer key for the mistakes planted in it.

17
00:01:18.275 --> 00:01:23.675
Thirty in total, and one practice is deliberately clean, to catch false alarms.

18
00:01:23.675 --> 00:01:28.775
A test regenerates everything and fails if the committed data has drifted.

19
00:01:29.375 --> 00:01:34.250
Let's follow one practice, Harborview Heart Clinic, a cardiology packet.

20
00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:39.300
Load packet reads the five files as lines, and compile packet walks them.

21
00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:43.075
Line twelve of the hours file says Friday, eight to three.

22
00:01:43.075 --> 00:01:54.650
A pattern match finds the day, parse time converts the times to twenty-four hour form, and the result is a day-hours object with the source hours.txt:12.

23
00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:59.075
Pydantic requires a source on every item, and checks its format.

24
00:01:59.675 --> 00:02:02.750
Now the compiled config goes to run rules.

25
00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:05.100
Take the hours conflict rule.

26
00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:13.100
It scans every line the agent might say, script lines and FAQ answers, for phrases like "our Friday hours are".

27
00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:15.825
Line nine of the script says eight to five.

28
00:02:15.825 --> 00:02:18.325
The official hours say eight to three.

29
00:02:18.325 --> 00:02:23.100
So it raises a high-severity issue, and cites both lines as evidence.

30
00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:28.525
For this practice the checks raise seven issues, matching its seven planted defects.

31
00:02:29.125 --> 00:02:33.075
The contradiction check has two backends with the same output.

32
00:02:33.075 --> 00:02:40.950
If an LLM model is configured and LiteLLM is installed, it asks the model for contradictions as JSON.

33
00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:45.475
That reply is validated, and every cited line must exist.

34
00:02:45.475 --> 00:02:49.000
If anything fails, it falls back to a keyword mock.

35
00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:54.150
The mock runs whenever no model is set, so nothing needs a key or a network.

36
00:02:54.750 --> 00:03:00.100
To run it today, install the requirements, run the generator, and run pytest.

37
00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:05.825
The thirty-nine tests include one per rule, and a golden config for the clean practice.

38
00:03:05.825 --> 00:03:12.625
In mock mode, on this synthetic data, the checks find all thirty planted defects with no false alarms.

39
00:03:12.625 --> 00:03:17.975
But there's no eval script yet, and synthetic defects are easier than real ones.

40
00:03:17.975 --> 00:03:19.500
Other limits:

41
00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:28.225
the packet format is a guess, the red-flag lists need a physician's review, and escalation fixes are flagged to always need a person.

42
00:03:28.225 --> 00:03:32.025
Next come repair, review, the smoke test and the eval.

43
00:03:32.625 --> 00:03:34.300
So, to recap.

44
00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:39.875
The compiler is deterministic, and every value points back to a file and a line.

45
00:03:39.875 --> 00:03:45.175
Nine rules and one semantic check turn mistakes into issues with evidence.

46
00:03:45.175 --> 00:03:49.025
A model is optional, validated, and never required.

47
00:03:49.025 --> 00:03:53.900
And the sample data carries its own answer key, so the checker can be scored.

48
00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:56.325
Thank you for watching.
